    "# Part 1: Project Background and Mathematical Definitions\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "This group project investigates aliquot sequences. An aliquot sequence is constructed by repeatedly calculating the sum of proper divisors of an integer. We will write code to generate aliquot sequences, classify their behaviour, study cycles such as amicable pairs, and visualise our results for a range of starting integers.\n",
    "\n",
    "All computations follow fixed stopping rules:\n",
    "- Maximum number of terms in one sequence: 30\n",
    "- Upper numerical threshold: if any term exceeds $10^9$, the sequence calculation terminates early.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Mathematical Definitions\n",
    "1. Sum-of-divisors function $\\sigma(n)$\n",
    "The divisor-sum function $\\sigma(n)$ returns the sum of all positive divisors of integer $n$, including $n$ itself.\n",
    "$$\\sigma(n)=\\sum_{d\\mid n} d$$\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Sum of proper divisors $s(n)$\n",
    "Proper divisors are positive integers less than $n$ which divide $n$.\n",
    "$$s(n)=\\sigma(n)-n$$\n",
    "Note that $n=1$ has no proper divisors, so $s(1)=0$.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Aliquot sequence\n",
    "Given a starting positive integer $k$, an aliquot sequence is defined iteratively:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\begin{cases}\n",
    "a_0 = k \\\\\n",
    "a_{i+1}=s(a_i)\n",
    "\\end{cases}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "We repeatedly apply the function $s$ to generate successive terms.\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Three possible behaviours of aliquot sequences\n",
    "Within our stopping constraints, every aliquot sequence has one of three outcomes:\n",
    "1. **Terminate**: The sequence eventually reaches 0. For example: $8 \\to 7 \\to 1 \\to 0$.\n",
    "2. **Enter a cycle**: Values start repeating. Once a value repeats, a cycle is detected.\n",
    "    - Cycle length 1: perfect numbers: $s(n)=n$\n",
    "    - Cycle length 2: amicable pairs e.g. $220 \\leftrightarrow 284$\n",
    "    - Cycle length $\\ge 3$: sociable numbers.\n",
    "3. **Unbounded growth**: Sequence terms keep increasing and exceed $10^9$. We stop before observing termination or a cycle.\n",
    "\n",
    "If none of these conditions are met after 30 terms, we also stop the computation.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. Perfect, abundant and deficient numbers\n",
    "We classify positive integers using $s(n)$:\n",
    "- Perfect number: $s(n)=n$ (sum of proper divisors equals itself)\n",
    "- Abundant number: $s(n) > n$ (sum of proper divisors is larger)\n",
    "- Deficient number: $s(n) < n$ (sum of proper divisors is smaller)\n",
    "\n",
    "## Project workflow\n",
    "This project is developed collaboratively using GitHub. Different group members implement functions, run batch computation, analyse cycles and produce visualisations in separate parts of this Jupyter notebook.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Task\n",
